WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.190
Follow the real request for July
second at eight, issued at seven,

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:08.290
with record identity thirteen
thousand and thirty six.

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:13.990
Its forty five column calendar vector
activates positions eight, twenty five,

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:14.990
thirty seven

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:15.990
and forty four:

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:19.010
hour eight, Monday, July

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:20.430
and working day.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:22.250
Holiday is zero.

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:27.200
These category definitions were fixed
before the final period rather than

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:28.730
learned from later outcomes.

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:30.630
Target hour weather,

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:34.830
rental components and observed rental
count remain outside the input.

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:38.910
The selected function uses
only hour and working day,

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:42.850
although the shared representation
contains additional calendar fields.

00:00:45.148 --> 00:00:49.508
The working day hour eight group contains
two hundred and forty eight training

00:00:49.508 --> 00:00:52.017
observations,
totalling eighty six thousand,

00:00:52.017 --> 00:00:54.838
six hundred and seventy seven rentals.

00:00:55.328 --> 00:00:59.488
Dividing gives three hundred and forty
nine point five zero four rentals per

00:00:59.488 --> 00:01:00.038
hour.

00:01:00.688 --> 00:01:02.338
This explains the saved parameter,

00:01:02.808 --> 00:01:06.658
but prediction simply looks it up;
it does not reread training outcomes.

00:01:07.708 --> 00:01:10.518
All forty eight possible
groups happen to exist here.

00:01:10.708 --> 00:01:15.188
A synthetic absent group check separately
verifies the training global mean

00:01:15.188 --> 00:01:15.938
fallback.

00:01:16.388 --> 00:01:20.498
A missing valid key and an invalid
request are different cases,

00:01:20.888 --> 00:01:24.798
so malformed inputs are rejected
rather than sent through that fallback.

00:01:26.900 --> 00:01:29.770
After all final forecasts are persisted,

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:34.640
the source join reveals five hundred and
sixty eight observed rentals for this

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:35.170
hour.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:40.080
Subtracting target from prediction gives
negative two hundred and eighteen point

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:41.470
four nine six.

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:46.966
The absolute error and underprediction
contribution are both two hundred and

00:01:46.966 --> 00:01:50.090
eighteen point four nine
six rentals per hour.

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:53.900
The sign tells us that the
saved level is too low,

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:57.890
and the squared error also contributes
to root mean squared error.

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:00.430
No gradient update follows.

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:02.850
We are evaluating a fixed function;

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:07.210
using this target in an update would make
the final outcome training information.

00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:15.170
The additive linear reference uses the
same vector but its saved training only

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:16.080
scaler.

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:18.040
For coordinate eight,

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:22.640
the active standardized value is
about four point seven seven six five.

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:28.548
The full standardized dot product is one
hundred and seventy one point two four

00:02:28.548 --> 00:02:29.540
zero four.

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:31.520
Adding the intercept,

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:34.430
one hundred and forty three
point seven nine four four,

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:38.820
gives three hundred and fifteen
point zero three four nine.

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:43.060
The preselected nonnegative
policy leaves it unchanged.

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:46.820
Centered inactive
coordinates also contribute,

00:02:47.210 --> 00:02:49.880
so summing only active
entries would be wrong.

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:55.000
Against five hundred and sixty eight, this
row misses more than the group forecast.

00:02:57.442 --> 00:03:01.852
The second predeclared example
changes to July fourth at eight.

00:03:02.262 --> 00:03:06.222
Its recorded holiday flag is
one and working day is zero,

00:03:06.222 --> 00:03:07.412
despite being a Wednesday.

00:03:08.142 --> 00:03:13.052
The key changes from hour eight,
working, to hour eight, nonworking.

00:03:13.642 --> 00:03:17.592
The saved mean is now seventy
six point four three four eight;

00:03:17.962 --> 00:03:20.172
the observed count is one
hundred and fifty three,

00:03:20.702 --> 00:03:24.632
giving residual negative seventy
six point five six five two.

00:03:25.582 --> 00:03:30.202
This response follows the original group
structure rather than a new holiday fit on

00:03:30.202 --> 00:03:31.132
final data.

00:03:31.602 --> 00:03:32.912
Calendar consistency

00:03:33.302 --> 00:03:37.442
and recorded holiday authority are
necessary to interpret that changed input

00:03:37.442 --> 00:03:38.132
correctly.

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:44.410
On December third at eight,
the ordinary working day key returns.

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:48.850
The forecast is again three hundred
and forty nine point five zero four.

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:51.890
The observed count is seven
hundred and thirty one,

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:54.400
producing a larger underprediction,

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:57.840
negative three hundred and
eighty one point four nine six.

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:02.330
July and December activate
different month coordinates,

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:04.930
but the selected model ignores month.

00:04:05.240 --> 00:04:09.790
This is an implemented property,
not evidence about why demand changed.

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:14.940
The additive model can lose on this one
request while scoring better in the final

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:15.550
aggregate.

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:18.130
One row explains a mechanism;

00:04:18.860 --> 00:04:22.710
thousands of aligned rows answer
the declared average error question.

